{
  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "3. Trust Nothing You Read",
  "description": "Every path in this tool comes out of a file someone else wrote. Where the code decides what a book is allowed to make it do.",
  "nextTour": "4. Names, Identity and Collisions",
  "steps": [
    {
      "title": "Two silent failures",
      "file": "epubconvert/source.py",
      "line": 4,
      "pattern": "Two conditions make a package impossible to convert into a readable book",
      "description": "A DRM-protected purchase produces a valid-looking archive nobody can open. A book iCloud has not downloaded produces one with empty files inside. Both pass every structural check, and both are discovered days later, on a device, by a reader.\n\nDetecting them turns that into a line in the run's output. Detection only — nothing here removes or circumvents protection; a protected book is reported and skipped."
    },
    {
      "title": "encryption.xml is not evidence of DRM",
      "file": "epubconvert/source.py",
      "line": 26,
      "pattern": "#: Encryption algorithms that are \\*not\\* DRM",
      "description": "The most expensive mistake available here, and it is easy to make: treat the presence of `META-INF/encryption.xml` as protection.\n\nBoth listed algorithms obfuscate embedded fonts, which is routine publishing practice. The book reads perfectly. In one real 2,805-book library, **every** package carrying `encryption.xml` used the Adobe font algorithm and not one was protected — so the naive check would have skipped a large slice of a normal library."
    },
    {
      "title": "Match by element, not by attribute",
      "file": "epubconvert/source.py",
      "line": 38,
      "pattern": "#: The element whose ``Algorithm`` attribute decides",
      "description": "`DigestMethod` and `CanonicalizationMethod` carry an `Algorithm` attribute too. Read those and a perfectly readable book is reported as DRM-protected.\n\nMatching is by **local name** (line 106), because packages differ in how they bind the xmlenc namespace.\n\nThe same paragraph explains why the file is parsed rather than pattern-matched: an attribute value may be single-quoted, which a text scan misses — and missing it reports a protected book as readable."
    },
    {
      "title": "Failing closed",
      "file": "epubconvert/source.py",
      "line": 82,
      "pattern": ":raises UnreadableEncryptionError: If the file exists but cannot be read",
      "description": "The direction of failure is the whole design here.\n\nA truncated `encryption.xml` is a realistic state for a partly synced iCloud file. Read that as \"no protection\" and the book is exported as an unopenable archive — which then sits in the output directory as a record of finished work that **no rerun will ever retry**.\n\nSo an unreadable encryption declaration means protected."
    },
    {
      "title": "And the caller agrees",
      "file": "epubconvert/source.py",
      "line": 130,
      "pattern": "    except UnreadableEncryptionError as exc:",
      "description": "`has_drm` catches the error and returns `True`. Fail-closed decisions are only worth making if every caller honours them; this is the one place that could have quietly undone the previous step."
    },
    {
      "title": "The undownloaded book",
      "file": "epubconvert/source.py",
      "line": 144,
      "pattern": "def has_dataless_files\\(package: Path\\) -> bool:",
      "description": "macOS sets `SF_DATALESS` (line 46) on a file whose contents live only in the cloud. One such file anywhere in the package and the export would be a book full of empty chapters.\n\nThis walks the whole package, which is the expensive operation on a cloud library, so it only runs when `--skip-incomplete` asks for it. `os.walk` is used rather than `rglob` plus `is_file` because it already separates files from directories, sparing a stat per entry."
    },
    {
      "title": "An honest bound",
      "file": "epubconvert/validate.py",
      "line": 36,
      "pattern": "#: Cap on the \\*stored\\* size of XML read from an archive",
      "description": "Note what this comment refuses to claim. It bounds the **read**, not the parse: ElementTree expands internal entities, so a small document can still expand to something far larger, and this limit does not prevent that.\n\nIt rejects implausible files early. It is not a memory bound, and saying so would be worse than not having it."
    },
    {
      "title": "A rootfile that leaves the archive",
      "file": "epubconvert/validate.py",
      "line": 154,
      "pattern": "def _checked_opf_path\\(full_path: str\\) -> str:",
      "description": "`container.xml` names the package document, and `container.xml` came from the book. Normalize the path, then refuse one that resolves outside the archive."
    },
    {
      "title": "What \"outside\" means",
      "file": "epubconvert/validate.py",
      "line": 187,
      "pattern": "def escapes_archive\\(path: str\\) -> bool:",
      "description": "Absolute, or `..`-prefixed after normalization. No archive member can match either, so inside a zip these are merely futile.\n\nThe docstring names the reason they are rejected rather than ignored: **the source side**. The cover extractor and the package reader join these onto the `*.epub/` directory on disk, where they reach real files that are no part of the book."
    },
    {
      "title": "Two checks, because neither covers the other",
      "file": "epubconvert/validate.py",
      "line": 206,
      "pattern": "def contained_file\\(",
      "description": "`escapes_archive` rejects the `../` and absolute paths. The resolved comparison on line 230 catches the other case: a symlink that sits inside the package but leads out of it.\n\nThis lives beside `escapes_archive` rather than beside its caller on purpose, so the containment rule is in one place. Any future reader that turns a manifest entry into a path on disk — fonts, thumbnails, metadata sidecars — should come through here rather than reinvent half of it."
    },
    {
      "title": "Remote is legitimate",
      "file": "epubconvert/validate.py",
      "line": 44,
      "pattern": "#: Href prefixes that name a resource outside the archive",
      "description": "The counterweight to the last three steps. The epub specification allows a remote resource in the manifest, so resolving one as an archive path would report a perfectly good book as missing a file.\n\nStrictness that fires on valid input is not strictness, it is a bug."
    },
    {
      "title": "The cover, and what a book could make it copy",
      "file": "epubconvert/inspect_output.py",
      "line": 57,
      "pattern": "The href is a value out of the book's own package document",
      "description": "`--covers` writes each book's cover image beside its archive. The href naming that image is attacker-controlled data.\n\nA manifest declaring `href=\"../../../secret\"` as its cover image would have this function read that file and write its bytes into the output directory — where they travel on to whatever device the shelf is copied to. Hence the `contained_file` call at line 75."
    },
    {
      "title": "with_suffix() would overwrite the book",
      "file": "epubconvert/inspect_output.py",
      "line": 84,
      "pattern": "# with_suffix\\(\\) \\*replaces\\* the extension",
      "description": "The second trap in the same function, and a good one.\n\n`Path.with_suffix()` **replaces** an extension. A cover href ending in `.epub` would resolve to the archive itself — and the freshly exported book would be overwritten with image bytes.\n\nThe name is built from the stem instead, and any path that is not a new file beside the archive is refused."
    },
    {
      "title": "Failures here are swallowed on purpose",
      "file": "epubconvert/inspect_output.py",
      "line": 99,
      "pattern": "    except \\(OSError, ValueError, ValidationError\\) as exc:",
      "description": "Writing a cover is a convenience, never the point of the run. By this point the book itself is complete and atomically in place.\n\nLetting a full disk or a rejected filename escape from here would abort the run and lose the counts for books that had already succeeded."
    },
    {
      "title": "The bug this module exists to catch",
      "file": "epubconvert/validate.py",
      "line": 8,
      "pattern": "That last check is the one that matters most here",
      "description": "A converter that silently drops a content file still produces a structurally valid **zip**. What breaks is the book, when a reader follows a manifest entry to a file that is not there.\n\nExactly that bug shipped in this project once. The module docstring says so, which is the right place for it."
    },
    {
      "title": "Everything the manifest promises",
      "file": "epubconvert/validate.py",
      "line": 429,
      "pattern": "def _check_manifest\\(members: set\\[str\\], package: Package\\) -> list\\[str\\]:",
      "description": "Manifest items must exist as members. Spine idrefs must exist in the manifest. Neither list may be empty.\n\nProblems are capped at five per category with an \"…and N more\" line, because a systematically broken book would otherwise print thousands."
    },
    {
      "title": "The stricter check, when asked",
      "file": "epubconvert/validate.py",
      "line": 475,
      "pattern": "def run_epubcheck\\(path: Path, timeout: int = 120\\) -> list\\[str\\]:",
      "description": "The external `epubcheck` tool, run through `subprocess` with a fixed executable resolved by `shutil.which`, no shell, and a timeout.\n\n`ValidationOptions.check` (line 89) only reaches it when the structural checks passed — no point running a two-minute validator over an archive already known to be broken. `cli.py` verifies the tool is on `PATH` at argument-parsing time, so the run fails before doing any work rather than on the first book."
    }
  ]
}
